. Norvane's 2027 capex is planned up 12%, and debt looks workable for it.\n- **Pellham's problem is equity, not debt.** Pellham plans 2027 capex up 18%, and it is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria. It withdrew a C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short. It now plans to cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes until financing improves. That suggests its plan needs an equity component that debt alone isn't replacing, probably because of leverage or ratings limits. The sources don't say this directly.\n- **Equity is weak across the sector.** Two other utility share offerings were also cut back in size. The sector can't count on equity to fill the gap.\n\n**Implication:** Debt markets can fund part of the 2027 capex, but not the full plan at the current mix. Expect Pellham's spending, and so its equipment orders, to come in below plan. Norvane's looks more secure.\n\n**Gaps in the evidence:** I found no data on issuer leverage, ratings headroom, debt maturities or Pellham's debt capacity. I also have only one Norvane bond as a data point. Treat the conclusion as moderately confident.","ttft_ms":3738,"type":"result","duration_ms":26412,"uuid":"a6987ae3-f1a1-465f-a2d6-b8582a67c3a3","ttft_stream_ms":3570,"time_to_request_ms":402,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":111,"system_prompt":18,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":8,"input_attachments":37,"input_hooks":10,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":12,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":39,"init_frame_send":12,"engine_pickup":4,"loop_entry":17,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":4,"autocompact":21,"request_announcements":38,"query_setup":12,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":20,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":5,"request_build":29},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":267,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":143,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":190,"process_anon_mib_turn_s